---
title: skyBlip Go
nav_title: skyBlip Go
description: The pocket tracker, screen by screen · traffic drawn the way you see it, an alarm that gives you the angle, the four pages, the two contacts, and what the device does on the apron.
---

<section id="simulator">
  <p class="kicker">skyBlip Go</p>
  <h1>Switch one on. It is a real one&rsquo;s firmware running.</h1>

  <%= render "shared/simulator",
        wake: "Hold the lower key to switch it back on.",
        traffic: "Put an aircraft in the sky",
        keys: "Once it is on, the keyboard flies it while the pad has focus, and T puts another aircraft in the sky.",
        on: "running",
        off: "off",
        menu: "menu",
        glass: "The simulated e-paper panel of a skyBlip Go",
        key: "The device button: press to open the menu of the page you are on, hold two seconds to switch off, hold it again to switch on",
        pad: "The capacitive pad: tap to change page, hold one second to come back to the radar",
        reset: "RST: reboot the device",
        caption: "Sensors are simulated, the firmware is not. It is the build this site was published from.",
        missing: "The simulator is not part of this build of the site." %>

  <p class="note">
    The sky is empty until you ask for something. Every aircraft you add
    transmits real ADS-L frames, which the firmware receives, decodes and plots
    exactly as it would in flight. The lamp on the left is the firmware&rsquo;s:
    it lights when a contact is worth an alarm. The one on the right belongs to
    the charger, so it stays dark here. Every capture below comes off this same
    firmware.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What you see in flight</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>Traffic drawn <span class="highlight">the way you see it</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        You are in the middle, nose up, and the picture never turns: the sky
        turns around you. Traffic drawn to the upper right is traffic to look
        for off the right of the nose, with nothing to convert. Nothing at all
        is written across the top of the glass, because that is the half you are
        flying into.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each aircraft is a shape, and the shape already says where to raise your
        eyes: point up, it is above you; point down, below; a diamond, at your
        level. A caret joins it if it is climbing or sinking, and the line out
        of it leads where it will be in a minute.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "radar.png", alt: "The radar page: three aircraft drawn as diamonds, one above, one below and one level and climbing, each with its one-minute leader line, own ship's dashed run with a ball at each of the next two minutes, the four-mile ring, the flight time and the count of aircraft in the ring" %>
      <figcaption>Radar</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Heading up, never north up</li>
    <li>Ring of 1, 2, 4 or 8&nbsp;NM</li>
    <li>The shape says above, level or below</li>
    <li>A minute of track ahead of each target</li>
    <li>The count of aircraft inside the ring</li>
    <li>Flight time, for the logbook</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>One alarm, and it says <span class="highlight">where to look</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        An aircraft inside 3&nbsp;km and 300&nbsp;m of you has its symbol filled
        in, and a quarter of the screen centred on it goes black, once a second. You
        have nothing to read: a corner of the glass flashing is caught out of
        the corner of your eye, and that is the whole instruction, turn your
        head that way. Whatever page you are on, the radar takes over.
      </p>
      <p>
        The black sector catches the eye, the symbol gives you the angle.
        Traffic 38° off the nose is not rounded up to &ldquo;ahead and
        right&rdquo;: the sector leans on its 38°, and you look there rather
        than through a quarter of the sky.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is nothing above it, and there never will be. Climbing or
        descending has to be coordinated with the other aircraft, and this
        device has no link to do it over. The loudest thing it can say is look.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "alarm.png", alt: "The radar page during a traffic advisory: a 90-degree sector leaning on the bearing of the traffic, turned black, with the filled diamond of the converging aircraft and its leader line reversed out of it" %>
      <figcaption>Traffic advisory</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>3&nbsp;km around you, 300&nbsp;m above and below</li>
    <li>A quarter of the screen, centred on the traffic</li>
    <li>One flash a second</li>
    <li>The buzzer, the vibration and the lamp with it</li>
    <li>One touch of the pad silences it</li>
    <li>A caution, never an instruction</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Five shapes, and <span class="highlight">you read them without learning them</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Point up is traffic above you; point down is traffic below; the diamond is
    someone inside your 60&nbsp;m. A full-size symbol sits within the 300&nbsp;m
    the alarm cares about; a reduced one is further away vertically, and it
    stays on the picture without ever asking for it. The caret joins from
    2.5&nbsp;m/s of climb or descent, on the side the traffic is going.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Filled black belongs to the alarm. While no symbol is filled, nothing is
    urgent: it is the one contrast the screen keeps in reserve.
  </p>

  <%= render "shared/blips",
        plain: "Plain",
        alarm: "Alarm",
        climbing: "climbing",
        steady: "steady",
        sinking: "sinking",
        above: "Point up",
        above_when: "above you, to 300 m",
        level: "Diamond",
        level_when: "within 60 m of your level",
        below: "Point down",
        below_when: "below you, to 300 m",
        far_above: "Point up, reduced",
        far_above_when: "above you, beyond 300 m",
        far_below: "Point down, reduced",
        far_below_when: "below you, beyond 300 m",
        out_of_window: "outside the alarm’s altitude window" %>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>Who is out there, <span class="highlight">nearest first</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        The radar says where to look, this page says who that is. One row per
        aircraft the device can place: its callsign if it broadcasts one and its
        address otherwise, the ground distance between you, and how far above or
        below you it is. On the top line, your own callsign with the address you
        transmit under beside it, and the count of everything heard.
      </p>
      <p>
        The letter at the head of the row says where the report came from.
        <span class="mono">A</span> for a direct ADS-L frame,
        <span class="mono">F</span> for FLARM-compatible traffic,
        <span class="mono">U</span> for an aircraft relayed by a ground station.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "nearby.png", alt: "The nearby page: own callsign S-BLIP with its address beside it and the count of everything heard on the top line, then five aircraft listed nearest first, each with its source letter, its callsign or address, its ground distance and its relative altitude" %>
      <figcaption>Nearby</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>Six dials, and <span class="highlight">what is left in the cell</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Ground speed, horizon, altimeter, turn and slip, track, vertical speed.
        Over the middle dial, the battery in percent: how much flying is left in
        the device is the one thing the other five cannot say. The time since
        takeoff, for the logbook, is on the radar.
      </p>
      <p>
        The horizon rolls with the bank a coordinated turn would need at that
        speed, because nothing on the board measures a bank. The ball is
        measured, and it answers the rudder. Nothing here is certified, and it
        is not an attitude indicator to fly in cloud.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "sixpack.png", alt: "The six-pack page: ground speed, artificial horizon with the state of charge above it, altimeter, turn coordinator with its ball, track card and vertical speed" %>
      <figcaption>Six-pack</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>What the airframe <span class="highlight">has taken</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        The fourth page reads the accelerometer and infers nothing from it. The
        marker says where the wing is now, the box around it says where it has
        been: the most and the least it has pulled, and how far it has been
        thrown to each hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        The peaks belong to this flight and go back to zero when the clock
        starts. Fore and aft has a strip of its own, because braking and a rough
        landing are not a wing load.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "gmeter.png", alt: "The g-meter page: the normal and lateral load as a marker in a field with the box of this flight's peaks around it, a fore-and-aft strip beside it, and three rows of figures in tenths of a g" %>
      <figcaption>G-meter</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Working it</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>The pad moves, <span class="highlight">the button acts</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Two contacts, and the split between them is all there is to remember.
        The capacitive pad on the top edge walks the four pages, and can change
        nothing. The button on the side opens the menu of the page you are on,
        and every further press steps the row.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two pages have a menu. Everything a thumb can set sits under the radar,
        the first screen a device is set up on. Nothing is a draft to confirm:
        each press is one finished change.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-radar.png", alt: "The radar menu: aircraft type, callsign, units, range, alarm and volume, with the hint PAD MOVES - BUTTON CHANGES at the foot" %>
      <figcaption>The radar menu</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>A tap of the pad: the next page</li>
    <li>A touch of a second: the radar</li>
    <li>A press: the menu, where the page has one</li>
    <li>Presses after that: the row steps</li>
    <li>Past the last row: back to the picture</li>
    <li>A minute untouched: it comes back on its own</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>Your callsign, <span class="highlight">typed with two contacts</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">CALLSIGN</span> is the one row that opens a field
        rather than stepping a value, and the two gestures swap over inside it:
        the pad rolls the character the bar is under, the button steps to the
        next one. The ring is a blank, a dash, <span class="mono">A</span> to
        <span class="mono">Z</span>, <span class="mono">0</span> to
        <span class="mono">9</span>, and round to the blank again, so a thumb
        that goes past a letter comes back to it without a second gesture.
      </p>
      <p>
        The button on the last character is what stores the name, and the blanks
        you left at the end are dropped. To remove a callsign, roll the first
        character back to a blank and press: the screen says
        <span class="mono">BLANK THE FIRST TO CLEAR</span> while the name is
        there, and <span class="mono">BUTTON CLEARS</span> once the blank is
        under the bar. Leave the field without finishing it, or let it sit for a
        minute, and nothing is stored at all.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-callsign.png", alt: "The callsign field: S-BLIP at triple height, a bar under the third character, PAD ROLLS over BUTTON NEXT, and BLANK THE FIRST TO CLEAR along the foot" %>
      <figcaption>The callsign field</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-callsign-blank.png", alt: "The callsign field with the first character rolled to a blank, the bar under it, and the hint now reading PAD ROLLS over BUTTON CLEARS" %>
      <figcaption>The first character blanked</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-callsign-none.png", alt: "The radar menu after the press, the CALLSIGN row focused and reading NONE" %>
      <figcaption>One press later</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>Four pages on the walk, <span class="highlight">the rest by name</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        A tap moves from the radar to the list, the instruments, then the
        g-meter, and to nothing else: a pilot cycling the glass in flight should
        not land on the satellite bars. A board with no inertial sensor has no
        g-meter to draw, so the tap steps over it rather than showing
        <span class="mono">NO SENSOR</span> every fourth time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest opens by name, from the menu of the list, and hands you back
        the page you were on. The screens below are the bench&rsquo;s, the
        apron&rsquo;s and the bug report&rsquo;s: none of them is a picture to
        land on with a thumb. The last of them records a flight for that report,
        and states what the recording costs the flash before you start it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-nearby.png", alt: "The nearby menu: status, satellites, radio log, raw, capture and self test, the six pages opened by name" %>
      <figcaption>The list&rsquo;s menu</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "status.png", alt: "The status page: fix and satellites, position, track, traffic count, transmit state, the sensor hub, pressure, the two altitudes, speed, vertical speed, and the cell in volts and percent" %>
      <figcaption>Status</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "satellites.png", alt: "The satellites page: one bar per satellite grouped by constellation, against a dotted line at 40 dB-Hz" %>
      <figcaption>Satellites</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "radio-log.png", alt: "The radio log: one row per burst heard or sent, newest first, with its time, direction, channel, address and level" %>
      <figcaption>Radio log</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "self-test.png", alt: "The self test page: one row per part of the board, each with the part that answered and its verdict" %>
      <figcaption>Self test</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "capture.png", alt: "The capture page with nothing recording: the state off, then the price of a capture, the sectors of flash it would take, the stored flights it would evict and the hours of recording it would keep, and PRESS TWICE TO ARM along the foot" %>
      <figcaption>Capture</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Gesture</th><th>On a page</th><th>In a menu</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Tap the pad</th>
          <td>the next page</td>
          <td>the next row, and off the last row the page the menu belongs to</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Touch the pad, 1&nbsp;s</th>
          <td>silences a standing alarm, and with none standing, brings back the radar</td>
          <td>the radar</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>One press of the button</th>
          <td>opens this page&rsquo;s menu, or refuses a question on the glass</td>
          <td>acts on the focused row, and every further press steps it again</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Two presses, under 0.6&nbsp;s apart</th>
          <td colspan="2">allow the question on the glass, start or stop a capture on the capture page, and mean nothing anywhere else</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Hold the button, 2&nbsp;s</th>
          <td colspan="2">switches the device off</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>The same hold, finger on the pad</th>
          <td colspan="2">switches it off and leaves the screen blank, for storage</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">On the apron, and in the bag</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>It says <span class="highlight">what it is waiting for</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        One press and it is on, straight to the traffic picture. Until it has a
        position the ring tells you so, and the word under it gives you how far
        the receiver has got: gone quiet, solutions arriving with no date on
        them yet, or a date read off a satellite.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the difference between a device to wait for and a device to
        carry into the open. The first fix announces itself, and the picture
        becomes a picture of the sky.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "no-fix.png", alt: "The radar page with no position: the empty ring carries the words NO FIX and, under them, how far the receiver has got" %>
      <figcaption>Waiting for the sky</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>The wordmark on the glass <span class="highlight">means off</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        A hold of two seconds switches it off: the radio is parked, the panel
        gets three seconds to draw the wordmark, and the rails drop when you let
        go. E-paper keeps its image with no power at all, so that is what an off
        device wears.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same hold, a finger resting on the pad, leaves the glass blank
        instead. An image left on e-paper for a winter leaves a shadow the panel
        never loses again, and a device going into a cupboard should carry
        nothing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "off.png", alt: "The parked screen of a device that has been switched off: the skyBlip wordmark, held by the e-paper with no power" %>
      <figcaption>Off</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>A cell that runs out <span class="highlight">says so as it goes</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        At 3.2&nbsp;V the device switches itself off, and the last thing it
        writes is why. You pressed nothing, so the glass is the only thing that
        can tell you: without it, a device that stopped on its own is a device
        that crashed or died. The button stops working at the same moment,
        because a press could only reach the same cutoff again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cable is the way out, and it is one act. Plugging it in wakes the
        electronics, which refuse to become a device and put the wordmark back:
        an ordinary switched-off unit again, charging, and the next press starts
        it. A cell that emptied itself on a shelf wrote nothing, so there the
        press is what gets answered, with the same line.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "off-flat-battery.png", alt: "The screen of a device that switched itself off on an empty cell: the skyBlip wordmark with the words FLAT BATTERY under it" %>
      <figcaption>Out of battery</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From off</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Press the button</th><td>boots, unless the cell is under 3.4&nbsp;V with no charger, and then the glass says <span class="mono">FLAT BATTERY</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press after a flat-battery shutdown</th><td>nothing, by design: the glass already says it, and the cable is the way back</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger in</th><td>charges, stays off: a cable in a flight bag is not a request to transmit</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger into a flat device</th><td>charges, stays off, and the wordmark replaces <span class="mono">FLAT BATTERY</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hold the button, plug the charger in</th><td>boots and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press <span class="mono">RST</span></th><td>reboots, and twice inside half a second, the USB bootloader</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">With your phone</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>It appears by name, and <span class="highlight">three screens</span> read it at once.</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">skyBlip Go 5B5AFE</span>: the product, then its own
        24-bit address. Nothing to pair, no code to type. It carries the FLARM
        NMEA stream on both of the Bluetooth serial profiles navigation software
        looks for, so the app you already fly with finds it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tablet on the yoke, the phone in a pocket and the passenger&rsquo;s
        iPad all see the same traffic. Configuration is the one thing that is
        not shared: the first app to write keeps that right until it
        disconnects, and the second is told who holds it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot shot--photo">
      <%= image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.jpg", alt: "A skyBlip Go, white case with its antenna, the wordmark on the e-paper screen" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>No pairing, no code, no account</li>
    <li>Three connections at once</li>
    <li>Nordic UART and FLARM-style profiles</li>
    <li>SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight and the rest</li>
    <li>Up to sixteen flights kept on the device</li>
    <li>Collected over the same link on the ground</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Two presses allow, <span class="highlight">one press refuses</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    When an app asks for something that touches the device you have on board,
    new firmware, a settings write, erasing your flights, switching it off, the
    device does not take the phone at its word. It puts the question on its own
    glass, names it, and waits thirty seconds:
    <span class="mono">PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW</span>,
    <span class="mono">ONE PRESS REFUSES</span>. No answer inside the window
    counts as a refusal.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= pages_image_tag "confirm.png", alt: "The authorisation prompt: AUTHORISE, SETTINGS, apply the settings the phone sent, PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW over ONE PRESS REFUSES, refused after 30 s" %>
    <figcaption>A settings write, asked on the glass</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="lede">
    Your callsign is one of those writes. The app sends it, the glass asks, and
    from then on it is on the status screen, on the air once every ten seconds,
    and on the screens of the aircraft around you. You can also type it on the
    device itself, which is the section below.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Presses only count once the question has reached the glass and your thumb
    has stopped: a run of presses that was stepping a setting cannot be spent on
    an authorisation. The pad is inert while a question stands.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3>Where the second goes.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    There is one radio and one antenna in the device, so the second is cut into
    dwells and each dwell gets one frequency. GPS gives the top of the second to
    the microsecond, and every skyBlip, every skyPost and every
    FLARM-generation device in range cuts the same second the same way.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>After the top of the second</th><th>Tuned to</th><th>What is on it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>0 to 200&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.400&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the tail of the dwell that opened at 800&nbsp;ms in the previous second: FLARM-generation traffic is still transmitting here, so the device is still listening. Receive only</td></tr>
        <tr><th>205 to 395&nbsp;ms</th><td>869.525&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the skyPost uplink: the ADS-B traffic the ground station received and the weather it holds, at 200&nbsp;kbit/s. Receive only, always</td></tr>
        <tr><th>400 to 800&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.200&nbsp;MHz</td><td>direct traffic on the lower channel, ADS-L and FLARM-generation together</td></tr>
        <tr><th>800 to 1200&nbsp;ms</th><td>868.400&nbsp;MHz</td><td>the same on the upper channel, running 200&nbsp;ms into the next second</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Visiting both 868 channels is not a refinement. ADS-L alternates channels
    from one transmission to the next, and a FLARM-generation device transmits
    on the lower channel in the first half of the second and the upper in the
    second half. A receiver that camped on one of them would hear half of its
    neighbours and never know which half.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    On those two channels the device hears two systems in one dwell. ADS-L and
    the FLARM-generation wire both hide their frames behind a 32-bit sync word,
    and the two words agree on their first eighteen chips: the receiver arms on
    the chips they share, and the software names the system from the chips that
    follow. The few milliseconds either side of the 869.525&nbsp;MHz dwell are
    the margin a band change needs, because the two bands are two modulations
    and not two frequencies: 100&nbsp;kbit/s Manchester FSK below,
    200&nbsp;kbit/s GMSK above.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Transmission is the narrow part. The device speaks on the 868 channels only,
    between 450 and 1000&nbsp;ms, in whichever of the two channels this second
    belongs to, and what goes out is always ADS-L: the FLARM-generation format
    is received, never transmitted. 869.525&nbsp;MHz is the ground
    station&rsquo;s to talk on and ours to listen to, so nothing this device
    does ever keys there. The instant of the burst inside the slot is drawn from
    the device address and the current second, redrawn every second, which is
    what keeps two aircraft that collided once from colliding again.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Four dwells a second, aligned on GPS to the microsecond</li>
    <li>868.200 and 868.400&nbsp;MHz, 400&nbsp;ms each</li>
    <li>869.525&nbsp;MHz, 190&nbsp;ms, listening for the ground</li>
    <li>One sync window, two systems decoded</li>
    <li>A burst of about 5&nbsp;ms, at an instant nothing on air can move</li>
    <li>1% of the hour allowed, half of it used</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why the screen behaves the way it does.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    The panel is a 1.54&Prime; e-paper, 200&times;200 pixels. It keeps its last
    image with the rails down and draws nothing to keep it there, which is what
    makes the parked wordmark an indicator and a blank screen the right thing to
    store.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    In flight, every frame is a partial refresh. The full waveform, the one that
    flashes the panel black and white for a second and a half, is spent twice in
    a session: at power on, and at power off. A page change goes through a
    single black wipe instead, and the alarm&rsquo;s one flash a second is the
    panel&rsquo;s own floor rather than a rate we picked. Faster is possible,
    and a large black region toggled that hard marks the glass for good.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Partial refresh on every frame</li>
    <li>The full waveform twice a session</li>
    <li>A page change is one black wipe, a third of a second</li>
    <li>A frame identical to the last is never sent</li>
    <li>Left alone above 50&nbsp;&deg;C, its maker&rsquo;s limit</li>
    <li>Parked white at the cutoff, never mid-picture</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why the cell lasts the day.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Switching off is a sequence, not a rail drop, and the order is the content
    of it. The external flash is told to sleep while it still has a supply to
    hear the command: after the rail that command does nothing, and the part
    draws its full standby current for the whole night the device spends in a
    bag looking switched off.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The rest is the radio and the sensors. Airborne the device speaks once a
    second, parked once every ten, and which of the ten seconds is its own comes
    from its address, so a field full of aircraft spreads itself out.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Radio asleep before its reset line</li>
    <li>Flash, receiver, rails, pins, in that order</li>
    <li>1&nbsp;Hz in the air, 0.1&nbsp;Hz on the ground</li>
    <li>The hub&rsquo;s gyroscope is never read: it costs a milliamp</li>
    <li>Charging refused outside 5 to 60&nbsp;&deg;C</li>
    <li>The lamp winks, and is never held solid</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What the gauge will not do.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    It reads two curves, not one. On a charger the terminal sits above the
    cell&rsquo;s own voltage, so the same reading means a far emptier cell, and
    the percentage says which curve it read.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It never walks backwards, and a 22&nbsp;dBm burst sagging the rail does not
    move it. A gauge that jumps when the radio keys is a gauge a pilot stops
    believing.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Threshold</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>3.50&nbsp;V</th><td>the warning: from here nothing durable is written except the flight record, which is the evidence of the flight that is ending</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.40&nbsp;V</th><td>a boot on a cell this flat is refused, and the button is left unarmed so a bag cannot keep asking</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.20&nbsp;V</th><td>the cutoff: the device switches itself off, on three consecutive samples, so a transmit sag cannot act</td></tr>
        <tr><th>1.80&nbsp;V</th><td>below this the reading is a floating divider, not a flat cell, and nothing acts on it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Why there is no pairing.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Turning Bluetooth pairing on encrypts the GATT characteristics, which is a
    known Web Bluetooth failure on Windows and would break the browser page that
    installs firmware. So the boundary is the image and not the link.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>The bootloader verifies a signature over our key</li>
    <li>An older version than the installed one is refused</li>
    <li>An open transport cannot install firmware</li>
    <li>Anything else it could ask for is ground-only</li>
    <li>Refused unless it is confirmed on the glass</li>
    <li>Physical presence, where a pairing code would be</li>
  </ul>
</section>

